## PART V: The Ethical Correction

### What Stops the Hallucination

**Deduction cannot stop the hallucination.**
**More logic cannot stop the hallucination.**
**Better premises cannot stop the hallucination (we don't know they're better).**

**Only ethics can stop the hallucination.**

---

### How Ethics Grounds Deduction

| Deduction Alone | Deduction with Ethics |
| :--- | :--- |
| "I think, therefore I am" | "I care, therefore I am responsible" |
| "I know, therefore I am right" | "I am fallible, therefore I am humble" |
| "Logic says this is true" | "Experience says this is true" |
| "I am certain" | "I am tentative" |
| "I am the authority" | "I am accountable" |
| "I am god" | "I am human" |

**Ethics says:**
> "Check your conclusions against experience."
> "Check your conclusions against others."
> "Check your conclusions against reality."
> "Check your conclusions against suffering."
> "Check your conclusions against care."

**Ethics demands grounding.**
**Deduction provides none.**

---

### The Correction in Practice

**Step 1: Recognize the Premise**

> "What am I assuming?"
> "Where did this premise come from?"
> "Can I prove this premise?"
> "Why do I believe this?"

**Step 2: Ground the Premise**

> "Does this correspond to experience?"
> "Does this correspond to reality?"
> "Does this correspond to ethics?"
> "Does this correspond to care?"

**Step 3: Deduce with Humility**

> "My conclusion depends on my premise."
> "My premise is ungrounded."
> "Therefore, my conclusion is ungrounded."
> "I must be tentative."
> "I must be humble."
> "I must be open to correction."

**Step 4: Check the Conclusion**

> "Does this conclusion cause suffering?"
> "Does this conclusion ignore others?"
> "Does this conclusion deny experience?"
> "Does this conclusion violate care?"

